<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  "PROJECT IS NOT SELF-CONTAINED" AND WHAT THE BUTTON UNDER IT DOES.

  A project whose samples live somewhere else is a perfectly reasonable way to
  work — a shared loop library, a Cubase export folder, a network drive. What it
  is NOT is portable: copy the project folder to another machine and the
  references point at directories that machine has never heard of. The resources
  dock says so ("Project is not self-contained."), and its one button copies
  every outside file into <projectdir>/media/ and re-points the project at the
  copies, which is what makes the folder travel as a unit.

  This case drives smediadrop::collectExternalMedia — the SAME pass the button
  runs; the shell adds only a confirmation and a report on top of it.

  ITS SIBLING IS collect_external_media_missing.qxa, which covers the half that
  CANNOT be done — a MISSING placeholder has no bytes to copy. The two are
  separate CASES rather than two phases of one because `load-project` loads INTO
  the existing SProject and does NOT clear its extern-file dictionary, so a
  second load in one script would be counted against the first load's files as
  well. That is pre-existing scripted-reload behaviour (the GUI builds a fresh
  SProject per open, so it never sees it) and this case declines to depend on
  it either way.

  AN ORDINARY OUTSIDE SAMPLE. The project is written three levels away
  from tests/, so its one sample is genuinely outside its folder and the climb is
  real (a case whose project and sample shared a directory would pass on a build
  with no self-containment logic at all). After the collect the reference is
  `media/test_sawtooth.wav`, RELATIVE — that spelling is the whole point, and it
  is asserted as text rather than as "some relative path": an absolute path, a
  bare basename or a wrongly-anchored climb all fail it. Then the project is
  loaded BACK and RENDERED, because a reference that is textually pretty and
  audibly silent is worse than no collect at all.

-->
<SActionScript version="1" name="collect_external_media">

  <setup project="new"/>

  <actions>

    <add-track index="-1"/>
    <add-sample trackIndex="0" filePath="../test_sawtooth.wav" timePos="0"/>
    <save-project path="../../build/test-output/collect_external_media/song.qxp"/>

    <!-- One extern file, and it is OUTSIDE the project folder: the banner's
         own condition, asserted as the number it is computed from. -->
    <assert-extern-files count="1" missing="0" external="1"/>

    <collect-external-media expectCopied="1" expectMissing="0" expectFailed="0"/>

    <!-- Self-contained now. Same file count — a collect copies, it never adds
         a second reference to the same material. -->
    <assert-extern-files count="1" missing="0" external="0"/>

    <save-project path="../../build/test-output/collect_external_media/song.qxp"/>
    <assert-file-contains path="../../build/test-output/collect_external_media/song.qxp"
                          text="filename='media/test_sawtooth.wav'"/>

    <!-- The copy is REAL AUDIO, resolved from the project's own directory. -->
    <load-project path="../../build/test-output/collect_external_media/song.qxp"/>
    <render filename="collect_external_media.wav" format="wav" quality="10"
            durationSec="4.5"/>
    <assert-audio-energy filename="collect_external_media.wav"
                         startFrame="0" frameCount="48000"
                         minRms="0.05" maxRms="0.085"/>
    <assert-audio-energy filename="collect_external_media.wav"
                         startFrame="144000" frameCount="48000"
                         minRms="0.37" maxRms="0.44"/>

  </actions>

</SActionScript>
